22:57:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, this isn't accurate as a description of the animal kingdom, and it's worth correcting because the real picture undercuts the "simple, universal nature" argument rather than supporting it. **Animal parenting strategies are wildly variable, not uniformly female-only:** - Emperor penguins: males incubate the egg alone for months in Antarctic winter while females go feed, then both share chick-rearing. - Seahorses: males carry the fertilized eggs and give birth. - Many bird species (like many raptors, geese, swans): both parents raise young together, sometimes with the male doing the bulk of feeding. - Wolves: pack-based, with multiple adults of both sexes helping raise pups. - Many primates (marmosets, titi monkeys): fathers do the majority of infant carrying. - Lions: females do most hunting *and* most cub care, while males primarily defend territory. - Many fish and amphibian species: males alone guard and tend the eggs/young while females leave entirely. - Plenty of species: neither parent sticks around at all (many reptiles, insects, fish) — offspring are on their own from birth. So "females raise young, males wander off" isn't a natural law—it's roughly true for *some* mammals (notably many that don't need paternal help to survive, like solitary cats), but it's one strategy among many that nature uses, not the default rule of biology. Nature is actually full of counterexamples to the story you're describing, including plenty of species where males are the dedicated caregivers. **Even within mammals, humans are unusual specifically because of biparental investment.** Human infants are exceptionally slow-developing and dependent for a very long time compared to other species—which is part of why human evolutionary history actually favored significant paternal involvement, grandparental help, and cooperative "alloparenting" (care from a wider group, not just mom) rather than a strict "mom does everything, dad wanders" model. Anthropologists studying hunter-gatherer societies (the closest living approximation to how humans lived for most of our history) generally find shared caregiving among mothers, fathers, older siblings, and grandparents — not a clean female-only default. So the "simple, instinctual, just look at nature" framing doesn't actually hold up once you look at nature closely — it's a selectively remembered version (lions, maybe some primates) that skips over the huge number of species that contradict it, including the ones most relevant to understanding human evolution specifically.
